# Python Standards Cursor Rules

## Code Style (PEP 8)

- Use 4 spaces for indentation, never tabs
- Maximum line length of 88 characters (Black formatter)
- Use snake_case for variables, functions, and modules
- Use PascalCase for classes
- Use UPPER_CASE for constants
- Use descriptive names, avoid abbreviations
- Add spaces around operators and after commas

## Type Hints

- Add type hints to all function parameters
- Specify return types for all functions
- Use Optional[] for nullable parameters
- Use Union[] for multiple possible types
- Use List[], Dict[], Tuple[] for collections
- Import types from typing module when needed

## Function Design

- Keep functions small and focused (max 20-30 lines)
- Use meaningful function names that describe what they do
- Limit function parameters (max 5-6 parameters)
- Use default parameters for optional arguments
- Return early to reduce nesting

## Documentation

- Write docstrings for all public functions and classes
- Use Google or NumPy docstring format
- Include parameter types and descriptions
- Document return values and exceptions
- Add examples for complex functions

## Error Handling

- Use specific exception types, not generic Exception
- Catch exceptions at the appropriate level
- Log errors with sufficient context
- Use try-except-else-finally when appropriate
- Raise custom exceptions when needed

## Import Organization

- Group imports: standard library, third-party, local
- Use absolute imports for external packages
- Use relative imports within the project
- Avoid wildcard imports (*)
- Import only what you need

## Code Organization

- Use __init__.py files for proper module structure
- Organize code by feature or domain
- Keep related functionality together
- Use classes to group related data and behavior
- Separate concerns (business logic, data access, UI)

## Performance

- Use list comprehensions for simple transformations
- Use generator expressions for large datasets
- Avoid unnecessary object creation
- Use appropriate data structures
- Profile code for bottlenecks

## Testing

- Write unit tests for all functions
- Use descriptive test names
- Test both success and error cases
- Use mocking for external dependencies
- Maintain good test coverage

## Code Quality

- Follow DRY principle (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- Use meaningful variable names
- Add comments for complex logic
- Refactor code regularly
- Use linting tools (flake8, black, mypy) 